+# R46 B7c — bounded host split with an explicit staging reserve (Opus 5 MEDIUM, kernel-maker)
+
+Inherited `git rev-parse HEAD` = **e11337c903f22618e332828dc6c023a2584ccb59** (B7b loader-wide
+allocation transaction), on top of `6d8ed4c6f` (per-buffer-type planner) and `a031ffef4` (B7a heap
+ledger). `git status --untracked-files=no` was **empty** at session start: every tracked file clean,
+only prior evidence directories and prompts untracked. Nothing is committed by this round.
+
+Rig: **AMD Radeon 780M Graphics, UMA, one Vulkan device**, host-visible heap 1 = 11,311,906,816 B.
+
+## 1. What the round had to close
+
+The R47d commission's last owed item: when a load's Vulkan groups cannot all be placed on
+device-local heaps, admit a **bounded** subset of bulk weight buffers onto host-visible memory rather
+than refuse — but only after a **staging reserve** is carved out first, so upload staging,
+`sync_staging`, prealloc scratch and compute buffers cannot be starved by weights. The unbounded
+placement that killed R47d P2/P4 at `submit seq=0` must remain impossible.
+
+## 2. Mechanism, and the ownership state of every byte
+
+### 2.1 Staging reserve
+
+| state | set by | owns | discharged by |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| unreserved | device before `ggml_vk_reserve_staging()` | nothing | — |
+| reserved | `vk_staging_reserve::try_reserve()` at device creation | `bytes` on the staging heap in the B7a ledger | `release()` in `~vk_device_struct` |
+| released | `release()` | nothing; `bytes` is zeroed **before** the ledger call | a second `release()` returns false and touches nothing |
+
+- It is a **ledger charge, not an allocation**. Nothing is allocated until staging is really needed,
+  and when it is, `ggml_vk_create_buffer` takes its own reservation on top. The reserve is therefore
+  a floor weights cannot eat, and it is deliberately **not** consumed by the real staging buffer —
+  releasing it there would hand the floor to whatever allocates next.
+- It is charged inside `ggml_vk_get_device()`, immediately after `createFence`, **before the buffer
+  type can plan or allocate anything**. On this rig the probe reads 1,073,741,824 B on heap 1 with
+  `reserved(heap 1) >= bytes` at that instant and no other reservation in existence.
+- Heap choice is not "some host-visible heap": it walks `sync_staging`'s own flag sets
+  (`HOST_VISIBLE|HOST_COHERENT|HOST_CACHED`, then `HOST_VISIBLE|HOST_COHERENT`), so the reserve lands
+  where staging really allocates.
+- If no host-visible heap can take it, one stderr line says so and the device continues with **no**
+  reserve; host placement then stays bounded by the ledger alone.
+
+### 2.2 Bounded split
+
+- A placement is a **host split** when the memory TYPE lacks `DEVICE_LOCAL` — per type, never per
+  heap, because one heap can carry both (R47d's small combined-property heap 2 is exactly that).
+- `vk_heap_ledger::reserve_plan()` gains `host_split_max` and `out_host_bytes`. The walk tracks the
+  bytes it has placed on host candidates and skips a candidate that would pass the bound exactly as
+  it skips one that does not fit its heap — so it keeps searching and backtracking rather than
+  refusing. Subtraction form only; nothing can wrap.
+- The **symmetry break now compares `host_split`** as well as heap and bytes. Without that, two
+  buffers with identical heaps/sizes but opposite host-ness would be called interchangeable and the
+  non-decreasing-choice restriction could prune the only assignment inside the bound — a refusal the
+  planner never proved. Covered by a test that fails without the flag.
+- **Spill selection rule (stated and tested): plan-order first-fit.** Device candidates come first
+  for every buffer, so the buffers that spill are the *suffix* of plan order; the walk backtracks
+  only when a prefix choice makes the remainder infeasible. Consequence, stated not hidden: llama's
+  plan order is not size-sorted, so the spilled suffix can hold large buffers while small ones stay
+  resident. That is the rule, not a defect.
+
+### 2.3 Wiring through the B7b transaction
+
+Nothing in `ggml/src/ggml-alloc.c` or `src/llama-model.cpp` changed. The backend answers
+`INFEASIBLE` when the bounded split still cannot place the set, so `ggml_backend_alloc_plan_commit()`
+emits the **same message family** as B7b — `cannot place all … this load needs` — releases every open
+plan and fails **before the first plan-owned driver allocation**. `could not decide placement for
+all` keeps its exact meaning (search bound). B7a/B7b rollback and exact-once release are untouched:
+the split is just which candidate an entry holds, and an entry is still either adopted by
+`plan_alloc_buffer` or released by `plan_free`, never both.
+
+One new line, stderr, ungated (a run that splits must say so with tracing off):
+
+```
+ggml_vulkan: bounded host split admitted: 2147483648 B of 24696061952 B placed on host-visible
+memory (bound 10238164992 B, staging reserve 1073741824 B on heap 1)
+```
+
+### 2.4 What is unchanged
+
+| case | why unchanged |
+|---|---|
+| non-Vulkan backends | no ggml-alloc / loader change at all; `plan_begin` is a Vulkan-side function |
+| single-buffer callers, mixed loads | untouched code paths; all 21 inherited `test-alloc` cases pass |
+| a load that fits device memory | candidate ORDER is untouched and nothing spills, so `host_bytes == 0` and the assignment is what B7b produced |
+| `GGML_VK_PLACEMENT=bulk-large-heap` | **candidate order is untouched** — `ggml_vk_placement_attempts()` still puts `DEVICE_LOCAL`-on-the-largest-heap first, and the receipt line is unchanged — so any load the policy previously placed device-local is byte-identical. It is **not** a device-only chain: it *prepends* its attempt and falls through to the stock chain ("a failure here is NOT fatal"), whose host-visible flag sets still produce host candidates. Those fallback placements are now bounded like every other host placement — which is exactly the R47d failure mode, bounded on purpose. |
+
+No performance code, no tuning, no kernel change.
